  <data key="d0">Comment for Appendix MS to Part 1024 - Mortgage Servicing Model Forms and Clauses</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df38a08194da67325630df5024565b9d</data>
  <data key="d2">Appendix MS-1 is part of the broader guidance offered in comments that determine how mortgage servicing should be documented.")</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="GFE">
  <data key="d0">GFE</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">GFE, or Good Faith Estimate, is a document that outlines the estimated costs and terms of mortgage loans, required to be provided to a borrower.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d42271ce6fb4dee8837d86cbc31e6d3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Good Faith Estimate (GFE)">
  <data key="d0">Good Faith Estimate (GFE)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The GFE is a document that provides borrowers with an estimate of the costs and fees associated with a mortgage loan, required under RESPA.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d42271ce6fb4dee8837d86cbc31e6d3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Balloon Payment">
  <data key="d0">Balloon Payment</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Balloon Payment is a larger-than-usual payment at the end of a loan, often due in mortgages that have low initial payments followed by high final payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d42271ce6fb4dee8837d86cbc31e6d3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Dealer">
  <data key="d0">Dealer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Dealer refers to a seller or contractor involved in property improvement loans or manufactured home sales, facilitating the connection between borrowers and lenders.&lt;SEP&gt;A dealer is an entity engaged in originating loans, including those to be consolidated by mortgage lenders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d42271ce6fb4dee8837d86cbc31e6d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Federal Government">
  <data key="d0">Federal Government</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Federal Government oversees various agencies and regulations related to housing and mortgage lending, including those that impact RESPA and Regulation X.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d42271ce6fb4dee8837d86cbc31e6d3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Effective Date of Transfer">
  <data key="d0">Effective Date of Transfer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Effective Date of Transfer refers to the date agreed upon for the transfer of servicing responsibilities for a mortgage loan between servicers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d42271ce6fb4dee8837d86cbc31e6d3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Federally Related Mortgage Loan">
  <data key="d0">Federally Related Mortgage Loan</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Federally Related Mortgage Loan is a type of mortgage loan that is either insured or backed by a federal agency or that involves a federal financial institution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d42271ce6fb4dee8837d86cbc31e6d3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Secretary of HUD">
  <data key="d0">Secretary of HUD</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Secretary of HUD is a government official responsible for overseeing housing development programs and policies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Federal National Mortgage Association">
  <data key="d0">Federal National Mortgage Association</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Federal National Mortgage Association, commonly known as Fannie Mae, is a government-sponsored enterprise that supports mortgage lending by purchasing residential mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Government National Mortgage Association">
  <data key="d0">Government National Mortgage Association</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Government National Mortgage Association, known as Ginnie Mae, is a U.S. government agency that backs mortgage-backed securities issued by approved lenders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation">
  <data key="d0">Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation, known as Freddie Mac, is a government-sponsored enterprise that provides liquidity to the mortgage market by purchasing home loans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer Credit Protection Act">
  <data key="d0">Consumer Credit Protection Act</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Consumer Credit Protection Act is a federal law aimed at protecting consumers in credit transactions and providing transparency in lending.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Home Equity Conversion Mortgage">
  <data key="d0">Home Equity Conversion Mortgage</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Home Equity Conversion Mortgage (HECM) is a type of reverse mortgage that allows seniors to convert part of their home equity into cash.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Department of Housing and Urban Development">
  <data key="d0">Department of Housing and Urban Development</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) is a U.S. government department responsible for national policies and programs related to housing needs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Creditors">
  <data key="d0">Creditors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Creditors are defined as entities or individuals that make or invest in loans, particularly in residential real estate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Residential Real Estate Loans">
  <data key="d0">Residential Real Estate Loans</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Residential real estate loans are loans secured by residential properties, such as single-family or multifamily units.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Broker">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Broker</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mortgage broker is an intermediary who connects borrowers with lenders, facilitating the mortgage loan process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Reverse Mortgage">
  <data key="d0">Reverse Mortgage</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A reverse mortgage is a financial product allowing homeowners, typically seniors, to convert home equity into cash without selling their home.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Loan Originator">
  <data key="d0">Loan Originator</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The loan originator is a professional, either a lender or a mortgage broker, responsible for initiating the loan process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d0">Mortgaged Property</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mortgaged property refers to the real estate being used as security for a mortgage loan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f06e0cc32de831cc9342a017ef34c1cb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Regulation Z">
  <data key="d0">Regulation Z</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Regulation Z is a federal regulation that governs the disclosure of information regarding consumer credit transactions, including periodic statements for residential mortgage loans.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation Z is a regulation that implements the Truth in Lending Act (TILA), ensuring that consumers are informed about the costs and terms of credit.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation Z refers to the regulations issued by the Bureau to implement the Federal Truth in Lending Act, focusing on consumer protection in credit transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af4c87cd145caa5dcae4e34a95edd62f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3f248b6dcc905cb618bc79b5abce479</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Truth in Lending Act">
  <data key="d0">Truth in Lending Act</data>
  <data key="d1">law</data>
  <data key="d2">The Truth in Lending Act is a federal law designed to promote informed use of consumer credit by requiring disclosures about its terms and cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Real Estate Settlement Procedures Act (RESPA)">
  <data key="d0">Real Estate Settlement Procedures Act (RESPA)</data>
  <data key="d1">law</data>
  <data key="d2">RESPA is a federal law that governs the settlement process for federally related mortgage loans, ensuring transparency and fairness in real estate transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Settlement Service">
  <data key="d0">Settlement Service</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Settlement services include a variety of services provided in connection with the transaction of federally related mortgage loans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)">
  <data key="d0">Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">FDIC is a U.S. government agency that provides deposit insurance to depositors in U.S. commercial banks and savings institutions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="National Credit Union Administration (NCUA)">
  <data key="d0">National Credit Union Administration (NCUA)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">NCUA is the government agency that regulates and supervises federal credit unions, ensuring their safety and soundness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Federal National Mortgage Corporation (FNMA)">
  <data key="d0">Federal National Mortgage Corporation (FNMA)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">FNMA, commonly known as Fannie Mae, is a government-sponsored enterprise that facilitates liquidity in the mortgage market by buying and guaranteeing mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation (Freddie Mac)">
  <data key="d0">Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation (Freddie Mac)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Freddie Mac is a corporation that buys and sells mortgages in the secondary mortgage market, promoting stability and affordability in housing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Title Company">
  <data key="d0">Title Company</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A title company is an institution qualified to issue title insurance and provide related services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Settlement Service Provider">
  <data key="d0">Settlement Service Provider</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A settlement service provider is an entity that provides various settlement services, including title insurance and property appraisals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Title Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Title Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Title insurance is a form of indemnity insurance that protects against financial loss from defects in title to real property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Special Information Booklet">
  <data key="d0">Special Information Booklet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The special information booklet is designed to help individuals understand the nature and costs of settlement services, published by the Bureau.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Table Funding">
  <data key="d0">Table Funding</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Table funding refers to a transaction where a loan is funded through contemporaneous advances rather than through secondary market transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Third Party">
  <data key="d0">Third Party</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A third party refers to any settlement service provider other than the loan originator involved in providing services in real estate transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Ability Determinations">
  <data key="d0">Ability Determinations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Ability determinations refer to assessments made to evaluate eligibility for services or benefits in various contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Title Commitments">
  <data key="d0">Title Commitments</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Title commitments are documents issued by a title company outlining the conditions under which title insurance will be issued.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Title Insurance Policies">
  <data key="d0">Title Insurance Policies</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Title insurance policies are contracts of insurance that protect against financial loss due to defects in real property title.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Notarization">
  <data key="d0">Notarization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Notarization is the process of verifying the authenticity of a document or signature by a qualified official.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Recordation">
  <data key="d0">Recordation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Recordation is the act of registering a document, such as a mortgage or title, in the public records to give it legal effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Rendering of Services">
  <data key="d0">Rendering of Services</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Rendering of services encompasses various professional activities such as legal advice, inspections, and appraisals related to real estate transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mortgage insurance protects lenders against losses resulting from a borrower's default on a mortgage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Settlement Agent">
  <data key="d0">Settlement Agent</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Settlement agents are professionals who facilitate the closing of real estate transactions, ensuring all documents are in order and funds are properly distributed.&lt;SEP&gt;The Settlement Agent is the individual or entity responsible for overseeing the closing of a real estate transaction, and their information must also be on the HUD-1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83b73ab7e1a0712a79396df18f6ec6fd</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Homeowner's Warranties">
  <data key="d0">Homeowner's Warranties</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Homeowner's warranties are service contracts that cover the repair or replacement of home systems and appliances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="ReSPA">
  <data key="d0">ReSPA</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">ReSPA, or the Real Estate Settlement Procedures Act, is a federal law that outlines regulations for closing real estate transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Contemporaneous Advance of Loan Funds">
  <data key="d0">Contemporaneous Advance of Loan Funds</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Contemporaneous advance of loan funds refers to the practice of providing loan amounts during a settlement at the same time the transaction occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="FDIC">
  <data key="d0">FDIC</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d2">The FDIC oversees aspects of financial institutions that could include servicers of federally related mortgage loans, particularly in the context of insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="NCUA">
  <data key="d0">NCUA</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d2">NCUA may regulate servicers connected to federally chartered credit unions involved in mortgage servicing.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="FNMA">
  <data key="d0">FNMA</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d2">Servicers manage loans that may be backed by FNMA, facilitating the mortgage process for borrowers.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Freddie Mac">
  <data key="d0">Freddie Mac</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d2">Servicers of mortgages must manage loans that can be sold to or guaranteed by Freddie Mac, ensuring adherence to guidelines.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="CFPB">
  <data key="d0">CFPB</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-202d2f76cd0f9edb21526229f52979b1</data>
  <data key="d2">The Bureau is a part of the CFPB, responsible for enforcing regulations like Regulation Z and RESPA.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Loan Amount">
  <data key="d0">Loan Amount</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918cc41243b0ac5553824466f93b99c3</data>
  <data key="d2">Mortgage insurance is often required based on the loan amount and borrower’s down payment, showing the direct relation between these financial elements.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Periodic Statement">
  <data key="d0">Periodic Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Periodic Statement is a regular financial document issued by a loan servicer to consumers, particularly those with mortgage loans. This statement provides a comprehensive overview of the consumer's loan account, detailing key information such as payment history, account status, amounts due, and any charges or adjustments made during a specific billing cycle. It includes summaries of account transactions and balances, as well as information about payments made, remaining balances, and due dates. The issuance of a Periodic Statement is a requirement under Regulation Z, ensuring that borrowers receive transparent and accessible documentation regarding their mortgage loan status and related activities. Overall, the Periodic Statement serves as an essential tool for borrowers to monitor their loan accounts and stay informed about their financial obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af4c87cd145caa5dcae4e34a95edd62f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60c4c20d8bc6a52cda7d60a42edba754&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a29965a27df3e0bbbe148a18921483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6301f087c4e151a23a8fbd945a680fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3f248b6dcc905cb618bc79b5abce479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6558cd155590495d4e8936a098dbb6b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc2767af1f1c3dce0807f3f719686f1a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Residential Mortgage Loan">
  <data key="d0">Residential Mortgage Loan</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A residential mortgage loan is a type of closed-end consumer credit transaction that is secured by a dwelling, requiring periodic statements under Regulation Z.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af4c87cd145caa5dcae4e34a95edd62f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Obligors">
  <data key="d0">Obligors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Obligors are individuals who are jointly liable for a mortgage loan, which impacts the delivery of periodic statements according to Regulation Z.&lt;SEP&gt;Obligors are individuals who are legally bound to take responsibility for a debt, particularly in joint loans or mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af4c87cd145caa5dcae4e34a95edd62f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3f248b6dcc905cb618bc79b5abce479</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Bi-weekly Billing Cycle">
  <data key="d0">Bi-weekly Billing Cycle</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A bi-weekly billing cycle is a payment schedule where payments are due every two weeks, which influences how periodic statements are formatted under Regulation Z.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af4c87cd145caa5dcae4e34a95edd62f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Credit Agreement">
  <data key="d0">Credit Agreement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A credit agreement outlines the terms of a loan, including the requirement to send periodic statements as per Regulation Z guidelines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af4c87cd145caa5dcae4e34a95edd62f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer">
  <data key="d0">Consumer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Consumer is defined as an individual who engages with services or products, particularly in the context of financial products such as loans and mortgages. This includes obtaining loans or mortgages, for which the Consumer is subject to specific terms, including periodic statements, payment obligations, and other financial responsibilities. Consumers are individuals who purchase goods or services for personal use and are afforded protection under various financial regulations, which encompass aspects related to mortgages.

In certain contexts, the Consumer is also involved in bankruptcy proceedings, where they maintain their mortgage loan status and obligations. Within this framework, the Consumer refers to individuals who may experience delinquency regarding their loans and are therefore in the process of fulfilling their payment responsibilities or managing their debts. Overall, a Consumer utilizes financial services, including mortgage loans for financing real estate purchases, and is granted information regarding their financial commitments, particularly during challenging financial situations such as bankruptcy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60c4c20d8bc6a52cda7d60a42edba754&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ac7ac60b5bdcc966bdd54287f029d40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6301f087c4e151a23a8fbd945a680fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6558cd155590495d4e8936a098dbb6b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ae377d836631c2c07f6ba5b136cf3ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc2767af1f1c3dce0807f3f719686f1a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Disclosure Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Disclosure Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Disclosure Requirements encompass the legal mandates for what information must be included in periodic statements to consumers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6301f087c4e151a23a8fbd945a680fd</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Loan Agreement">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Loan Agreement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Mortgage Loan Agreement outlines the terms, conditions, and obligations of both the borrower and lender in mortgage transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6301f087c4e151a23a8fbd945a680fd</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Electronic Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Electronic Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Electronic Distribution refers to delivering periodic statements to consumers through digital means, subject to their consent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6301f087c4e151a23a8fbd945a680fd</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Temporary Loss Mitigation Program">
  <data key="d0">Temporary Loss Mitigation Program</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Temporary Loss Mitigation Program offers consumers relief options during financial hardship, impacting how payments are processed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6301f087c4e151a23a8fbd945a680fd</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="January 1">
  <data key="d0">January 1</data>
  <data key="d1">date</data>
  <data key="d2">January 1 is used as a reference date for transaction activities and periodic statements in the context of mortgage loans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="§ 1026.41(d)">
  <data key="d0">§ 1026.41(d)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">§ 1026.41(d) outlines specific regulations related to mortgage loan disclosures, including amounts due and explanations of payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer Finance">
  <data key="d0">Consumer Finance</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Consumer Finance oversees regulations that govern periodic statements related to mortgage loans and consumer protection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Payments">
  <data key="d0">Payments</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Payments refer to the financial transactions made by consumers towards their mortgage loans, including amounts that may be accelerated or temporarily modified.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Activity">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Activity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transaction activity encompasses all recorded financial transactions related to the mortgage loan since a specified date, in this case, January 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Transaction activity includes all transactions affecting the due amount on a loan, such as payments received, fees charged, and changes in account balances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Acceleration">
  <data key="d0">Acceleration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Acceleration is a financial term and process in mortgage lending that allows the servicer to demand the entire balance due when certain conditions are met, often changing the payment obligations of the borrower.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Late Payment Fee">
  <data key="d0">Late Payment Fee</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A late payment fee is a charge imposed on the borrower if they fail to make a payment by the due date.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Loan Modifications">
  <data key="d0">Loan Modifications</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Loan modifications involve changes made to the original mortgage loan terms, typically designed to make the loan more affordable for the borrower.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64d35f2de347c55b0effa77ac26cf9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Suspense Account">
  <data key="d0">Suspense Account</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A suspense account is a temporary account used to hold funds that cannot be immediately applied to a specific debt or payment, as evidenced by the payments mentioned in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Loan Transaction Activity">
  <data key="d0">Loan Transaction Activity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Loan transaction activity refers to all transactions that affect the amount due on a loan, including payments made, fees applied, and account balances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Outstanding Principal Balance">
  <data key="d0">Outstanding Principal Balance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The outstanding principal balance is the remaining amount of money that is owed on a loan, excluding interest and other charges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Current Interest Rate">
  <data key="d0">Current Interest Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The current interest rate is the rate at which interest is accrued on a loan, which can change based on the terms of the loan and market conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Suspense or Unapplied Funds Account">
  <data key="d0">Suspense or Unapplied Funds Account</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A suspense or unapplied funds account is used to hold payments that cannot yet be applied to a specific payment obligation, often due to partial payments or processing delays.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Delinquency Information">
  <data key="d0">Delinquency Information</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Delinquency Information refers to the details regarding a consumer's missed loan payments, including the length of delinquency and its implications.&lt;SEP&gt;Delinquency information pertains to the status of a consumer's payments, particularly when payments are overdue by a certain period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Toll-Free Telephone Number">
  <data key="d0">Toll-Free Telephone Number</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A toll-free telephone number provided by financial institutions allows consumers to easily access information regarding their accounts without incurring charges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Electronic Mailing Address">
  <data key="d0">Electronic Mailing Address</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An electronic mailing address provided by financial institutions for consumers to contact them regarding financial inquiries or account information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Prepayment Penalty">
  <data key="d0">Prepayment Penalty</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A prepayment penalty is a fee that some lenders charge if a borrower pays off their loan early, potentially affecting consumer choices regarding mortgage repayments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af08dffacc55223531b7b223d44e95df</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Delinquent Consumer">
  <data key="d0">Delinquent Consumer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Delinquent Consumer is an individual who has failed to make one or more required payments on their loan by the due date.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Reverse Mortgages">
  <data key="d0">Reverse Mortgages</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Reverse Mortgages are a type of home loan that allows homeowners to borrow against the equity in their home, typically exempt from certain consumer regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Reverse mortgages are specialized loans allowing homeowners to convert part of their home equity into cash, not counted in small servicer evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;Reverse Mortgages are loans for homeowners aged 62 and older that allow them to convert part of their home equity into cash, included in Money Topics by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.&lt;SEP&gt;Reverse Mortgages are loans for homeowners aged 62 and older that allow them to convert part of their home equity into cash, included in Money Topics by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.&lt;SEP&gt;Reverse Mortgages are loans for homeowners aged 62 and older that allow them to convert part of their home equity into cash, included in Money Topics by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-728d4db6e62d4fda79187b89fc62841e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Timeshare Plans">
  <data key="d0">Timeshare Plans</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Timeshare Plans involve shared property ownership, and specific financial requirements may exempt them from standard consumer protections.&lt;SEP&gt;Timeshare plans are arrangements where multiple parties hold ownership or rights to property for specified times, affecting mortgage servicing criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Coupon Book">
  <data key="d0">Coupon Book</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Coupon Book is a booklet containing individual payment coupons for a mortgage, allowing consumers to make payments conveniently.&lt;SEP&gt;A Coupon Book is a method of payment where a consumer receives a booklet with coupons for each billing cycle, used for making loan payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6558cd155590495d4e8936a098dbb6b4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Loan Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Loan Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Mortgage Loan Obligation is a binding agreement where a consumer borrows money to purchase real estate and must repay it with interest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Escrow">
  <data key="d0">Escrow</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Escrow involves an account where funds are held by a third party until the terms of a contract are met, often applicable in real estate transactions to cover taxes and insurance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Foreclosure">
  <data key="d0">Foreclosure</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Foreclosure is a legal process in which a lender takes possession of a property when the borrower does not make mortgage payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Judicial Foreclosure">
  <data key="d0">Judicial Foreclosure</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Judicial Foreclosure is a foreclosure process that occurs through court proceedings rather than through a non-judicial process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Judicial Foreclosure">
  <data key="d0">Non-Judicial Foreclosure</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-Judicial Foreclosure is a process allowing lenders to foreclose on a property without going through the court system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edd2b6454dad4b91c08c0e822e7052a9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Small Servicer">
  <data key="d0">Small Servicer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A small servicer is defined by regulations and is eligible for exemptions based on the number of mortgage loans serviced and their ownership status.&lt;SEP&gt;Small servicer is a classification for servicers that handle a limited number of mortgage loans, specifically 5,000 or fewer, under certain conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Small servicer refers to a mortgage loan servicer that manages a limited number of loans, specifically 5,000 or fewer, and meets specific criteria regarding ownership or origination of those loans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Loans">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Loans</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mortgage loans are financial products used by consumers to purchase real estate, which may or may not be considered in determining servicer qualifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortgage loans are loans secured by collateral of real estate property, commonly used for purchasing homes.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortgage loans are loans specifically for the purchase of real estate, where the property serves as collateral for the loan.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortgage loans are types of loans specifically secured by real estate, typically used for purchasing residential properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Credit Union">
  <data key="d0">Credit Union</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A credit union is a member-owned financial cooperative that provides a variety of banking services, including mortgage loans, to its members.&lt;SEP&gt;A credit union is a member-owned financial cooperative that provides various financial services, including mortgage loans, and meets specific criteria to qualify as a small servicer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Delineation of Small Servicer">
  <data key="d0">Delineation of Small Servicer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A delineation of small servicer refers to the specific terms and conditions under which a mortgage servicer qualifies as a small servicer, as outlined by regulatory standards.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Regulation 1026.41">
  <data key="d0">Regulation 1026.41</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Regulation 1026.41 is a section of the US Code of Federal Regulations that provides guidelines for mortgage servicing practices and consumer protections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="50 States">
  <data key="d0">50 States</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The 50 States are the political regions that make up the United States, where financial regulations are implemented and vary based on state law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer Protection Regulations">
  <data key="d0">Consumer Protection Regulations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Consumer protection regulations are laws designed to ensure the rights of consumers, particularly in financial transactions, preventing fraud and abuse.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f49562ee16f1eb1fb67b975d9a6dad22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Bank Holding Company">
  <data key="d0">Bank Holding Company</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A bank holding company is a corporation that owns or controls one or more banks and manages subsidiaries involved in mortgage loans and servicing operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Nonbank Servicer">
  <data key="d0">Nonbank Servicer</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A nonbank servicer refers to a financial entity that services mortgage loans but does not hold a banking charter, allowing it to operate under different regulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Nonprofit Entity">
  <data key="d0">Nonprofit Entity</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A nonprofit entity is an organization that does not operate for profit and may service mortgage loans on behalf of associated nonprofit entities.&lt;SEP&gt;A nonprofit entity provides various services, including mortgage loan servicing, and must comply with specific regulations to qualify as a small servicer.&lt;SEP&gt;A nonprofit entity that services mortgage loans with a tax exemption ruling under section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code and is involved in loan servicing activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Small Servicer Exemption">
  <data key="d0">Small Servicer Exemption</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The small servicer exemption is a regulatory provision that allows small servicers to be exempt from certain mortgage servicing requirements, provided they meet specific criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Servicing Rights">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Servicing Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mortgage servicing rights are the rights to service mortgage loans, which involve collecting payments and managing the loan's life cycle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Component Servicer">
  <data key="d0">Component Servicer</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A component servicer assists in mortgage servicing functions but does not engage in direct servicing activities as defined by regulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Master Servicer">
  <data key="d0">Master Servicer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A master servicer is the entity that holds the right to perform mortgage loan servicing and may do so directly or through a subservicer.&lt;SEP&gt;A master servicer oversees the management of mortgage servicing rights and may own or oversee multiple subservicers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0031a6554d7d6f650917d185dca826a6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Affiliate">
  <data key="d0">Affiliate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An affiliate refers to entities that are connected through specific relationships, often involving control or ownership dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Wholly Owned Subsidiary">
  <data key="d0">Wholly Owned Subsidiary</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A wholly owned subsidiary is a company whose entire stock is owned by another company, often managing specific functions like servicing loans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-485591d7ed5bd27f959024cd0494df93</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Housing Finance Agency">
  <data key="d0">Housing Finance Agency</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Housing Finance Agency is defined in federal regulations and plays a role in administering mortgage loans and assistance programs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Associated Nonprofit Entities">
  <data key="d0">Associated Nonprofit Entities</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Associated nonprofit entities operate under a common name or trademark to support a shared charitable mission or purpose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Loans">
  <data key="d0">Loans</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Loans refer to the sum of money that is borrowed, particularly mortgage loans referenced in the context of servicing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Affiliates">
  <data key="d0">Affiliates</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Affiliates are organizations related to the servicer, which may assist in servicing mortgage loans or have financial relationships with the servicer.&lt;SEP&gt;Affiliates refer to organizations associated with servicers that may also service loans or share operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Revenue Service (IRS)">
  <data key="d0">Internal Revenue Service (IRS)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The IRS is the U.S. government agency responsible for tax collection and tax law enforcement, including issuing tax exemption rulings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Small Servicer Definition">
  <data key="d0">Small Servicer Definition</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The small servicer definition outlines the criteria under which a servicer is classified as a small servicer, primarily based on the number of mortgage loans serviced.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0550a546818c80b0e01ac4500123fb4b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Bankruptcy">
  <data key="d0">Bankruptcy</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Bankruptcy is a legal proceeding involving a person or business that is unable to repay outstanding debts.&lt;SEP&gt;Bankruptcy is a legal process through which consumers can eliminate or repay their debts, triggering specific exemptions for mortgage servicing.&lt;SEP&gt;Bankruptcy is a legal process through which individuals or businesses can seek relief from some or all of their debts when they are unable to repay them.&lt;SEP&gt;Bankruptcy is a legal process through which individuals or businesses who cannot repay their debts can seek relief from some or all of their obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Bankruptcy is a legal status of a person or entity that cannot repay debts to creditors, which involves processes to relieve debts and manage reorganization of payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-60c4c20d8bc6a52cda7d60a42edba754&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6558cd155590495d4e8936a098dbb6b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc2767af1f1c3dce0807f3f719686f1a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Loans Exemptions">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Loans Exemptions</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mortgage loans exemptions refer to specific types of loans that are not considered in the determination of servicer qualifications, affecting their compliance with regulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Compensation">
  <data key="d0">Compensation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation refers to payments received by servicers for managing loans, affecting how certain loans are classified under regulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Periodic Statements">
  <data key="d0">Periodic Statements</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Periodic Statements are documents provided by loan servicers that detail the status of a loan, including payments due, amounts past due, and other relevant financial details.&lt;SEP&gt;Periodic statements are documents that servicers send to borrowers detailing their loan status, which may be affected by exemptions in servicing rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ae377d836631c2c07f6ba5b136cf3ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Home Equity">
  <data key="d0">Home Equity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Home equity represents the difference between the market value of a property and the amount owed on the mortgage, relevant for reverse mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Servicing Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Servicing Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Servicing requirements are regulations and rules governing how servicers must manage mortgage loans, including consumer communication and reporting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Voluntary Serviced Mortgage Loans">
  <data key="d0">Voluntary Serviced Mortgage Loans</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Voluntary serviced mortgage loans are loans that a servicer manages without receiving compensation and are treated differently under regulatory guidelines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7b68da8c1ffbbe5a866c5a2cb00a315</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="§ 1026.41">
  <data key="d0">§ 1026.41</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">§ 1026.41 is a regulation section related to mortgage loans and consumer protections in bankruptcy circumstances.&lt;SEP&gt;§ 1026.41 refers to a section in consumer financial regulations that delineates requirements related to disclosures in bankruptcy cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac7ac60b5bdcc966bdd54287f029d40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="United States Code">
  <data key="d0">United States Code</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States Code contains the laws of the United States and includes regulations referred to in the bankruptcy context.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States Code is the codification of federal statutory laws in the United States, including bankruptcy laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ae377d836631c2c07f6ba5b136cf3ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Period Statement">
  <data key="d0">Period Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Periodic Statement is a document issued by a loan servicer detailing the status of a borrower's mortgage loan account.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer Finance Protection Bureau">
  <data key="d0">Consumer Finance Protection Bureau</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Consumer Finance Protection Bureau is an agency of the United States government responsible for consumer protection in the financial sector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="§ 1026.41(e)(5)">
  <data key="d0">§ 1026.41(e)(5)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">§ 1026.41(e)(5) details specific consumer protections and requirements for servicers in relation to mortgage loans during bankruptcy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Spouse">
  <data key="d0">Spouse</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Spouse is an individual who is married to another and may share financial obligations, including mortgage loans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Bankruptcy Plan">
  <data key="d0">Bankruptcy Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Bankruptcy Plan is a legal document that outlines how the debtor will repay creditors after filing for bankruptcy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bankruptcy Plan outlines the terms under which a debtor will repay creditors, which may affect the payment structure of post-petition payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16a29965a27df3e0bbbe148a18921483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Court">
  <data key="d0">Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Court is a judicial body that resolves legal disputes, including those related to bankruptcy cases and mortgage loans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Consumers Statement of Intention">
  <data key="d0">Consumers Statement of Intention</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Consumer's Statement of Intention is a filing in bankruptcy proceedings that identifies the consumer's intent regarding property securing a mortgage loan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Periodic Statement or Coupon Book">
  <data key="d0">Periodic Statement or Coupon Book</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Periodic Statement or Coupon Book refers to the documentation provided by servicers detailing the status of a mortgage loan account and payment schedules.&lt;SEP&gt;A periodic statement or coupon book is a document provided by the servicer, detailing payment schedules, amounts due, and other mortgage-related information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc2767af1f1c3dce0807f3f719686f1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter 13 Bankruptcy">
  <data key="d0">Chapter 13 Bankruptcy</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter 13 Bankruptcy allows individuals to propose a repayment plan to make installments to creditors over three to five years.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 13 Bankruptcy is a form of bankruptcy that allows individuals to reorganize their debts and establish a repayment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 13 Bankruptcy is a legal process that allows individuals with regular income to create a plan to repay all or part of their debts over a period of time while retaining ownership of certain assets, such as a home.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f3f248b6dcc905cb618bc79b5abce479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ae377d836631c2c07f6ba5b136cf3ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-179537b4c45f08e77c69e828197fb96c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
</node>
